\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on three English IE benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} contains 551 test instances annotated for joint NER (6 types) and RE (7 types).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} provides 3{,}453 test sentences with 4 entity types, where models achieve ${\approx}$91\% micro F1.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} consists of 1{,}287 social media instances with emerging entities, used to probe method boundaries (52\% zero F1).

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
Both \textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are fine-tuned with LoRA (rank$=$32, $\alpha{=}64$) for 5 epochs using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} under identical training procedures, ensuring comparisons across the two model families (both at 8B scale) reflect model differences rather than training confounds.
Qwen3-8B's SciERC checkpoint has a minor train--test mismatch: empty \texttt{<think></think>} blocks (4--5 tokens, no reasoning content) were included in training but are suppressed by constrained decoding at inference; CoNLL and WNUT-17 are unaffected.
A quantitative analysis rules out this confound (Appendix~\ref{app:thinking_token}).
We draw $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary; single seed, seed${=}42$), with $N{=}16$ for scaling experiments.\footnote{$N{=}8$ establishes the primary baseline at minimal compute cost; $N{=}16$ with multiple seeds verifies scaling behavior. $N$-scaling analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}) shows sublinear gains beyond $N{=}8$. $N{=}16$ results in Table~\ref{tab:robustness} (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}); 3-seed stability ($\sigma{\leq}0.026$; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}, Appendix~\ref{app:multiseed}) confirms identical signal rankings across seeds.}
Quality estimation uses Spearman $\rho$ and AUROC; downstream utility uses best-of-$N$ selection macro F1 (instance-averaged) with exact span matching (NER) and head-tail-label matching (RE).
Inference uses vLLM with constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}
\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Quality estimation across tasks and models (Spearman $\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, seed${=}42$). Best value per column in \textbf{bold}. SciERC evaluates joint NER (6 types) and RE (7 types, single benchmark---preliminary); CoNLL evaluates NER (4 types). $N{=}16$ results appear in Table~\ref{tab:robustness} (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}).}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC NER}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC RE}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8} \cmidrule(lr){9-10}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & \textbf{.663} & \textbf{.350} & .694 & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .650 & .250 & .670 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .602 & .275 & .609 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & .614 & .134 & \textbf{.706} & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .266 & .601 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_signal_heatmap}
\caption{Quality estimation heatmap (Spearman $\rho$) across five signals and four configurations. Left: full evaluation set; right: conditional set (non-zero F1 instances). On the full set, structural signals dominate on SciERC NER while all signals converge on CoNLL. On the conditional set, EM emerges as the strongest signal on both NER ($\rho{=}0.360$) and RE ($\rho{=}0.417$), while LP RE collapses to near-zero ($\rho{=}0.011$).}
\label{fig:signal_heatmap}
\end{figure*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents rank correlation ($\rho$) and AUROC for five confidence signals across tasks and models.\footnote{$\varepsilon{=}0.05$ is a convenience threshold used only in the selection analysis (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}); quality estimation metrics ($\rho$, AUROC) are continuous and $\varepsilon$-free. Selection F1 is stable across $\varepsilon \in [0, 0.2]$ (Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon_robustness}).}

\paragraph{Difficulty-dependent signal complementarity.}
On SciERC NER (6 types), structural signals lead: VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $\rho{=}0.360$, both outperforming surface ($\rho{\leq}0.295$) and LP ($\rho{=}0.205$); on RE, VC extends this advantage ($\rho{=}0.350$; preliminary), but the remaining hierarchy reverses: multi-seed analysis shows LP rises above both SJ and FK (Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}), likely because RE's 40\% zero-F1 rate creates a binary-outcome regime where generation confidence outperforms structural overlap.
The best NER AUROC reaches 0.816 (EM, CoNLL Qwen), indicating strong binary discriminative power; pairwise bootstrap CIs confirm the SJ--LP gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$, CI excludes zero) but the SJ--EM gap is marginal (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).

\paragraph{$\rho$--AUROC dissociation.}
Signal choice depends on use case: on CoNLL, EM leads AUROC (0.816) despite a middling $\rho$ (0.451), preferred for binary triage; this dissociation replicates across configurations (LP leads AUROC at 0.761 on CoNLL LLaMA yet trails in $\rho$).

\paragraph{Signal rankings shift with task difficulty.}
On CoNLL (greedy F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads ($\rho{=}0.451$, AUROC${=}0.816$); this crossover---EM${>}$SJ on easy tasks vs.\ SJ${>}$EM on hard tasks---demonstrates difficulty-dependent complementarity, consistent across model families.
On WNUT-17 (52\% zero F1), full-set VC achieves $\rho{=}0.724$ but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$ (n.s.; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP exhibits strong \textit{negative} correlation ($\rho{=}{-}0.410$); all conditional signals collapse, suggesting a capability boundary beyond which consistency captures binary success/failure but not quality gradation.

\paragraph{Conditional filtering.}
Restricting to non-zero F1 instances reshuffles signal rankings (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}, right): EM emerges as the strongest conditional signal on both NER ($\rho{=}0.360$) and RE ($\rho{=}0.417$; preliminary), while structural signals drop substantially (VC NER: $0.379{\to}0.300$) and LP RE collapses to $\rho{=}0.011$ (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection F1 on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). NER: 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$ ($n{=}551$), Fisher combined $p$ vs.\ greedy; RE: single seed (preliminary). Best non-oracle in \textbf{bold}. $^\dagger$Greedy: a fixed $T{=}1.0$ sample (not $T{=}0$), serving as the no-selection reference. VC selection uses majority-threshold voting ($\text{freq}{>}N/2$; see \S\ref{sec:signals}).}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ & RE F1 \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.778 \pm 0.003$ & --- & 0.585 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{Per-sample signal}} \\
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 & 0.395 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{Instance-level signals (consensus-proximity selection)}} \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .68 & 0.386 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 & --- \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & .45 & 0.372 \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & .26 & 0.359 \\
\midrule
Greedy$^\dagger$ & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- & \textbf{0.404} \\
Random           & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- & 0.354 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Despite strong quality estimation, confidence signals yield limited gains for best-of-$N$ selection (Table~\ref{tab:selection}; see also Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}).
Four of five signals are instance-level by construction (they assign identical scores to all candidates); the non-trivial question is whether LP---the only per-sample signal---can exploit the oracle headroom.
We anchor on SciERC, where output diversity is substantial (39--43\% unique outputs; Appendix~\ref{app:diversity}).

\paragraph{No significant improvement over greedy.}
We evaluate significance via 2-seed instance-level sign tests ($n{=}551$, Fisher combination).
On NER, the oracle (0.778) reveals 13.3~pp of headroom above greedy (0.645).
LP achieves the highest mean selection F1 (0.647), indistinguishable from greedy ($\Delta{=}{+}0.2$~pp, $p{=}0.29$); all other signals also fail to reach significance (all $p{>}0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:selection}), with 75--80\% of instances producing identical F1 under signal selection and greedy.
On RE, the best signal (LP, 0.395) falls below greedy (0.404, $-0.9$\,pp; single seed, preliminary), while 18.1~pp of oracle headroom remains.
VC, the best quality estimator ($\rho{=}0.379$), selects to only 0.623 on NER ($\Delta{=}{-}2.2$~pp, $p{=}0.26$); the direction is consistent with CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}), though CoNLL's limited oracle headroom (${\leq}4.0$~pp at $N{=}8$) constrains selection comparison.

\paragraph{Quality estimation $\neq$ output selection.}
Instance-level signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} is best; when per-sample variance is low, even perfect quality estimation fails to discriminate.
Individual seeds exhibit notable variance (even the best seed $p{=}0.19$), motivating multi-seed evaluation.
Closing this gap likely requires per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency-based signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ and show sublinear gains from $N{=}8$ to $N{=}16$ (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}, Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}); LP exhibits the largest temperature sensitivity on NER ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.044$), with the signal hierarchy preserved across temperatures.
NER signals are reasonably calibrated (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}); RE miscalibration (ECE up to 0.339, preliminary) imposes a ceiling at $\rho{\approx}0.28$ for $N{=}16$.

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Self-consistency---sampling multiple outputs and aggregating them---has become a powerful strategy for improving language model reasoning.
On discrete-answer tasks such as arithmetic and commonsense QA, majority voting over chain-of-thought samples yields large gains: +18 percentage points on GSM8K (PaLM-540B; \citealp{wang2023selfconsistency}).
More broadly, test-time compute scaling through repeated sampling and selection has emerged as a complementary axis to model scaling, offering substantial quality improvements at inference time \cite{snell2025scaling, brown2024monkeys}.
These results rest on a shared assumption: that among $N$ stochastic samples, a selection mechanism can identify better outputs.
For tasks with a single correct answer, this assumption holds naturally---the majority vote is the selector.
But information extraction (IE) poses a structurally different challenge.
The output is not a single token or number; it is a \textit{set} of entities or relations with internal dependencies, where two samples may extract overlapping but non-identical structures.
There is no obvious majority to vote on, partial agreement is the norm rather than the exception, and the space of valid outputs is combinatorially large.
Can self-consistency still provide useful signal in this structured setting?

Prior work on confidence estimation for LLM-based IE has conflated two distinct objectives: \textit{quality estimation} (which inputs does the model handle reliably?) and \textit{sample selection} (which of $N$ outputs is best?).
These require different signal properties: the former benefits from instance-level aggregation, while the latter demands per-sample discrimination.
Self-consistency methods for discrete answers \cite{wang2023selfconsistency, chen2024usc} and weighted variants \cite{taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty} assume outputs can be grouped by equivalence---an assumption that breaks for entity sets with partial overlap.
The most relevant study, \citet{yao2026consistency}, examines consistency--accuracy correlations for LLM-based IE, finding results that our quality-estimation analysis corroborates.
Our work complements theirs in three directions: (i)~structural matching beyond surface-level agreement, (ii)~testing whether quality estimation translates to best-of-$N$ selection (it does not), and (iii)~a difficulty gradient revealing non-obvious findings invisible on a single dataset.

All experiments use constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}, the standard for schema-compliant IE output.\footnote{Our free-form ablation (\S\ref{sec:ablation}) confirms fine-tuned models produce 99.95\% valid JSON without grammar constraints.}

Our central finding is the \textbf{correlation-selection gap} (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}): consistency signals achieve strong quality estimation ($\rho$ up to 0.45) but fail at sample selection despite substantial oracle headroom.
Four of five signals are instance-level by construction; log-probability, the only per-sample signal, is too compressed to discriminate (\S\ref{sec:discussion}), though where compression is mitigated, selection recovers gains (\S\ref{sec:ablation}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_overview}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap. \textbf{Left}: high inter-sample agreement correlates with high F1 (quality estimation). \textbf{Right}: instance-level signals assign identical scores to all candidates, precluding sample selection.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We systematically compare five confidence signals from three families---structural (entity voting confidence [VC], Soft Jaccard [SJ]), surface (exact match [EM], Fleiss' $\kappa$ [FK]), and model-internal (log-probability [LP])---across NER and RE on SciERC \cite{luan2018scierc}, CoNLL-2003 \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll}, and WNUT-17 \cite{derczynski2017wnut}, using Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B.
Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Metric dissociation.}
    We find that quality estimation, binary discrimination, and sample selection decouple, so single-metric evaluation risks selecting the wrong signal.

    \item \textbf{The correlation-selection gap.}
    Despite strong quality estimation, no signal significantly improves sample selection: LP scores are too compressed within instances, and MBR-style selection correlates with F1 across but not within instances.

    \item \textbf{Difficulty- and task-dependent signal rankings.}
    Signal rankings shift with task complexity and type, and collapse at the capability boundary. RE conclusions rest on SciERC alone (preliminary).
\end{enumerate}
